      "heading": "What are people searching for when they look for online therapy?",
      "body": "<p><strong>People searching for online therapy in 2026 ask four kinds of question: which platform is best, what it costs, whether insurance covers it, and whether it is legitimate. A smaller, growing group asks whether an app or a chatbot can replace a therapist. Each question has a different intent, and a page that answers one of them badly will not rank for the others.</strong></p>\n<p>We built this section from two sources. The first is a scan of current search results and AI-generated answers for the core terms, carried out in November 2026. Those results were uneven in quality, and several were affiliate roundups or vendor blogs, so we used them to see which platforms and questions recur, not as evidence for any claim. The second is our own Search Console data from the last several months, which shows what people actually click on this site. Where the two disagree, we trust the clicks.</p>\n\n<h3>The platforms that recur in current results</h3>\n<p>Across the results we reviewed, the same names came up repeatedly: betterhelp, Talkspace, Brightside Health, Cerebral, Grow Therapy, Thriveworks, Online-Therapy.com and ReGain. Brightside and Cerebral are described as combining therapy with psychiatric medication management. Grow Therapy and Thriveworks appear most often in insurance-focused roundups. ReGain is the name most often attached to couples therapy.</p>\n<p>We verify and review three of these platforms: betterhelp, Talkspace and headspace. We have not verified the others' prices, insurance networks or outcomes to the standard we apply here, so we do not rank them. The scope section below explains the scope in more detail, and you should treat any ranking that puts an unverified platform alongside these three with the same caution we do.</p>\n\n<h3>The query clusters</h3>\n<p>The table groups the queries by intent. The \"Best page on this site\" column shows where we answer each one. Where a query has no strong answer on the site yet, the table says so.</p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Query cluster</th><th>Representative queries</th><th>Searcher intent</th><th>Best page on this site</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Best platform</td><td>best online therapy; best online therapy 2026; best online therapy app</td><td>Compare and choose</td><td>This guide; <a href=\"/\">homepage ranking</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cost</td><td>how much does online therapy cost; talkspace cost; betterhelp cost; online therapy cost per week</td><td>Price the decision</td><td><a href=\"/articles/online-therapy-cost\">Online therapy cost</a>; platform cost guides</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Insurance</td><td>does insurance cover online therapy; does talkspace take insurance; online therapy that takes insurance</td><td>Find out what they will pay</td><td><a href=\"/articles/does-insurance-cover-online-therapy\">Insurance guide</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Legitimacy</td><td>is betterhelp legit; betterhelp scam</td><td>Check for risk before paying</td><td><a href=\"/articles/is-betterhelp-legit\">Is betterhelp legit?</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Alternatives</td><td>alternatives to betterhelp; betterhelp alternatives</td><td>Switch or compare</td><td><a href=\"/articles/betterhelp-alternatives\">betterhelp alternatives</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Psychiatry</td><td>online psychiatry; psychiatrist vs online therapy apps</td><td>Get medication support</td><td><a href=\"/best-online-psychiatry\">Best online psychiatry</a>; <a href=\"/articles/online-psychiatry\">online psychiatry guide</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Free and low-cost</td><td>free online therapy; free therapy near me</td><td>Find something affordable</td><td><a href=\"/free-online-therapy\">Free online therapy</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Local</td><td>therapy near me; online therapist in [state]</td><td>Find a licensed clinician locally</td><td><a href=\"/online-therapy\">State pages</a>; <a href=\"/articles/online-therapy-near-me\">near me explainer</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Audience</td><td>online family therapy; online couples therapy; online therapy for women</td><td>Find a fit for a relationship or group</td><td><a href=\"/online-family-therapy\">Family therapy</a>; <a href=\"/online-therapy-for-women\">Online therapy for women</a></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Emerging</td><td>AI therapy app; can a chatbot replace a therapist</td><td>Evaluate AI as an alternative</td><td><a href=\"/articles/ai-chatbot-vs-therapist\">AI chatbot vs therapist</a></td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>What our own search data shows</h3>\n<p>Several earlier Search Console snapshots shared for this site show the shape of real demand. These are figures from the reports you shared, and they change over time, so read them as direction, not as a forecast.</p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Query or page signal</th><th>Position</th><th>Impressions</th><th>What it tells us</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>headspace online</td><td>About 8.5</td><td>About 204</td><td>Strong brand-led demand; the headspace review must answer the app-versus-therapy question directly</td></tr>\n<tr><td>how much does talkspace cost</td><td>About 9.7</td><td>About 35</td><td>Price intent is close to page one; the verified price table is the advantage</td></tr>\n<tr><td>alternatives to betterhelp</td><td>About 21</td><td>About 45</td><td>Switching intent; the alternatives guide should lead with reasons for leaving</td></tr>\n<tr><td>online psychiatry</td><td>About 76</td><td>About 58</td><td>High-value medical intent that is still far from page one; needs authority and links</td></tr>\n<tr><td>state pages (aggregate)</td><td>Roughly 60 to 90</td><td>Several thousand</td><td>Large volume at low positions; the state pages are the biggest long-term opportunity</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The pattern is consistent. Brand and price queries are close to page one because the site has specific, verified answers to them. Clinical and local queries sit lower because they need time, links and depth. That is why this guide is built around the three verified platforms and their prices, rather than around a long list of names we cannot stand behind.</p>\n\n<h3>How people phrase the question matters</h3>\n<p>Question-form queries are growing. People ask \"does insurance cover online therapy\" or \"can I get therapy without insurance\" rather than typing a category. That shapes how we write headings: each main section opens with a direct answer to the question it carries, so an answer engine can lift one sentence and a reader can find the next one in the same place. The same logic runs through every page on the site.</p>"
