      "heading": "#1 betterhelp: best for flexible talk therapy",
      "body": "<p><strong>betterhelp is a subscription talk-therapy platform. After an intake questionnaire it matches you with a licensed therapist, usually within days, and you can talk by live video, phone or chat, with messaging between sessions. Switching therapists is free. It has no psychiatry, and its self-pay price is quoted at signup rather than published, so the honest way to compare it is on the trade-offs below.</strong></p>\n\n<h3>Verified and company-reported facts</h3>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Figure or fact</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Therapists in network</td><td>31,739</td><td>Company-reported; not independently audited</td></tr>\n<tr><td>People helped</td><td>More than 6.6 million</td><td>Company-reported</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Time to match</td><td>As little as 2 days</td><td>Company-stated</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Session formats</td><td>Video, phone, live chat; messaging between sessions</td><td>Platform information</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Insurance</td><td>Accepted via coverage check; copays cited from around $23 per session</td><td>Company-cited; confirm with your plan</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Self-pay</td><td>Quoted at signup, varies by location and preferences; financial-aid questionnaire</td><td>Not independently verified by us</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Trustpilot</td><td>3.9 across 9,652 reviews</td><td>Captured August 2026</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Regulatory history</td><td>2023 FTC order over sharing health questionnaire data with advertisers</td><td>Documented; see privacy section</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h3>Strengths</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Breadth and speed.</strong> The largest network in the category makes it the most likely platform to find a clinician with the specialty or background you want, quickly.</li>\n<li><strong>Format flexibility.</strong> You can choose video, phone or chat for each session, which helps on days when video feels like too much.</li>\n<li><strong>Easy switching.</strong> Reviewers who changed therapists described it as straightforward, which matters because the first match is often not the final one.</li>\n<li><strong>Extra structure.</strong> Several reviewers in our sample described group sessions, worksheets and mood tools as useful add-ons.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Trade-offs</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No psychiatry.</strong> If medication may be part of your care, betterhelp is the wrong first choice, and coordinating an outside prescriber is the most common friction point in online care.</li>\n<li><strong>Cost is not published.</strong> The price varies with your location and preferences, and a reviewer couple in our sample reported paying about $600 combined per month. Check the quote and the cancellation terms before you enter a card.</li>\n<li><strong>Privacy history.</strong> The 2023 FTC order concerned advertising data sharing. The company changed the practices at issue, but it is a fact any buyer should weigh.</li>\n<li><strong>Messaging terms should be confirmed.</strong> One reviewer in our sample said messaging had been discontinued, while two others describe using it this autumn. Check what your plan includes before you depend on it.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Who should choose betterhelp</h3>\n<p>betterhelp suits adults who want talk therapy quickly, value live-session flexibility, expect they may need a different therapist before finding the right one, and either have insurance that covers the platform or can afford the quoted subscription with financial aid. It is a weaker fit if you need medication management or if the subscription model will tempt you to pay for sessions you do not attend.</p>"
