      "heading": "A short glossary of terms used in this guide",
      "body": "<p><strong>The vocabulary of online therapy mixes clinical, insurance and subscription terms. These definitions match how they are used here.</strong></p>\n<dl>\n<dt>Asynchronous messaging</dt><dd>Written exchanges with your therapist that do not happen in real time. You write when you want, and your therapist responds within a stated schedule.</dd>\n<dt>Copay</dt><dd>A fixed amount you pay per session when a provider is in your insurance network. Online therapy copays are typically $0 to $30 on in-network platforms.</dd>\n<dt>Deductible</dt><dd>The amount you pay in full each plan year before cost-sharing starts. It may or may not apply to therapy visits.</dd>\n<dt>EAP</dt><dd>Employee assistance program. A confidential employer benefit that often includes a set number of free counseling sessions per issue per year.</dd>\n<dt>EOB</dt><dd>Explanation of benefits. The insurer's statement of what was billed, allowed, paid and owed. It is not a bill.</dd>\n<dt>HIPAA</dt><dd>The US federal law that sets privacy and security rules for health information held by providers, insurers and their business associates.</dd>\n<dt>In-network</dt><dd>A provider with a contract with your insurer, which sets the price you pay and bars balance billing beyond the agreed amount.</dd>\n<dt>Licensed clinician</dt><dd>A mental-health professional holding an active state license, such as a licensed clinical social worker, licensed professional counselor, licensed marriage and family therapist, or licensed psychologist.</dd>\n<dt>Parity</dt><dd>Federal rules requiring that coverage for mental-health care be no more restrictive than coverage for medical care.</dd>\n<dt>Psychiatric prescriber</dt><dd>A physician or psychiatric nurse practitioner who can diagnose and prescribe medication. Therapists cannot prescribe.</dd>\n<dt>Superbill</dt><dd>An itemized receipt from a licensed provider, used to claim out-of-network reimbursement from an insurer.</dd>\n<dt>Trustpilot \"Invited\" tag</dt><dd>A label showing that a company invited the customer to leave a review. It does not mean the review is negative or positive.</dd>\n</dl>"
